нти́ровать"}, {"language": {"code": "es", "name": "Spanish"}, "word": "cementar"}, {"language": {"code": "es", "name": "Spanish"}, "word": "encementar"}, {"language": {"code": "sw", "name": "Swahili"}, "word": "simiti"}, {"language": {"code": "sv", "name": "Swedish"}, "word": "cementera"}, {"language": {"code": "cy", "name": "Welsh"}, "word": "smentio"}, {"language": {"code": "yi", "name": "Yiddish"}, "word": "צוקלעפּן"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To overlay or coat with cement.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["to cement a cellar floor"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, figurative) To unite firmly or closely.", "tags": ["figuratively", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "For they have entertained cause enough\nTo draw their swords: but how the fear of us\nMay cement their divisions and bind up\nThe petty difference, we yet not know.", "reference": "c. 1606–1607 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Anthonie and Cleopatra”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act II, scene i]:"}, {"text": "Olympic Games. — Besides the ordinary confederacies that join independent states together, a singular federal bond is remarkable in the Olympic games, which for many ages cemented the Grecian commonwealths by a joint tie of recreation and religious ritual.", "reference": "1840, John Dunlop, The Universal Tendency to Association in Mankind. Analyzed and Illustrated, London: Houlston and Stoneman, page 103:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(figuratively) To make permanent.", "tags": ["figuratively"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "But friendſhip is a calm and ſedate affection, conducted by reaſon and cemented by habit; ſpringing from long acquaintance and mutual obligations; without jealouſies or fears; and without thoſe feveriſh fits of heat and cold, which cauſe ſuch an agreeable torment in the amorous paſſion.", "reference": "1758, David Hume, “Essay XXII. Of Polygamy and Divorces.”, in Essays and Treatises on Several Subjects, new edition, London: Printed for A[ndrew] Millar, in the Strand; and A. Kincaid and A. Donaldson, at Edinburgh, →OCLC, page 115:"}, {"text": "[Dele] Alli’s ability to break forward from midfield was a prominent feature and the 19-year-old must have gone a long way to cementing his place in the team.", "reference": "2016 March 27, Daniel Taylor, “Eric Dier seals England’s stunning comeback against Germany”, in The Guardian, London, archived from the original on 22 Apr 2016:"}, {"text": "Me and Mr. Wade, we are good friends. My respect for him has grown over these seven weeks of attacks. We are very good friends. I think but for these attacks, it would have been a friendship that, as life goes, we would have stopped having. I think that you have cemented that we'll be friends to the day we die.", "reference": "2024 February 15, Fani Willis, 38:21 from the start, in See Fani Willis' entire defiant testimony in stunning courtroom moment, MSNBC, archived from the original on 16 Feb 2024:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "cmn", "name": "Chinese Mandarin"}, "word": "鞏固 /巩固"}, {"language": {"code": "da", "name": "Danish"}, "word": "cementere"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "zementieren"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "bebetonoz"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "megszilárdít"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "megerősít"}, {"language": {"code": "it", "name": "Italian"}, "word": "cementare"}, {"language": {"code": "it", "name": "Italian"}, "word": "consolidare"}, {"language": {"code": "ja", "name": "Japanese"}, "word": "固く結ぶ"}, {"language": {"code": "pt", "name": "